if (!validation.valid && disposition === "EVALUABLE_PASS") { +if (!validation.valid) { receipt.disposition = validation.disposition; receipt.pass = false; receipt.classification = CLASSIFICATION_FOR_DISPOSITION[validation.disposition] ?? "invalid-schema"; return { receipt, exitCode: exitCodeForDisposition(validation.disposition), }; } 7. Bounded verification Create a lane-local bounded runner: Bashcat > "$LANE/00-run-bounded.py" <<'PY' #!/usr/bin/env python3 from __future__ import annotations import datetime as dt import json import os from pathlib import Path import signal import subprocess import sys import time if len(sys.argv) < 4: raise SystemExit("usage: 00-run-bounded.py <seconds> <output-stem> <command...>") seconds = int(sys.argv[1]) stem = Path(sys.argv[2]) command = sys.argv[3:] stem.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True) started_wall = dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc) started = time.monotonic() process = subprocess.Popen( command, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True, start_new_session=True, ) timed_out = False try: stdout, stderr = process.communicate(timeout=seconds) except subprocess.TimeoutExpired: timed_out = True os.killpg(process.pid, signal.SIGTERM) try: stdout, stderr = process.communicate(timeout=5) except subprocess.TimeoutExpired: os.killpg(process.pid, signal.SIGKILL) stdout, stderr = process.communicate() ended_wall = dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc) exit_code = 124 if timed_out else int(process.returncode) Path(f"{stem}.stdout.log").write_text(stdout) Path(f"{stem}.stderr.log").write_text(stderr) receipt = { "schemaVersion": 1, "command": command, "timeoutSeconds": seconds, "startedAt": started_wall.isoformat(), "endedAt": ended_wall.isoformat(), "durationMs": round((time.monotonic() - started) * 1000), "timedOut": timed_out, "exitCode": exit_code, } Path(f"{stem}.json").write_text(json.dumps(receipt, indent=2) + "\n") print(json.dumps(receipt)) raise SystemExit(exit_code) PY chmod +x "$LANE/00-run-bounded.py" Required checks V1 — Owner-map parser and real glossary Bashpython3 "$LANE/00-run-bounded.py" 30 \ "$LANE/raw/verification-owner-map-self-test" \ python3 -B scripts/validate-owner-map-paths.py --self-test Expected: exit 0, message that all self-tests passed. Bashpython3 "$LANE/00-run-bounded.py" 60 \ "$LANE/raw/verification-owner-map-glossary" \ python3 -B scripts/validate-owner-map-paths.py \ --json-out "$LANE/raw/owner-map-glossary.json" \ GLOSSARY.md Expected: exit 0; otherwise record each exact path finding without modifying GLOSSARY.md. V2 — Trust-core Bun tests Bashpython3 "$LANE/00-run-bounded.py" 240 \ "$LANE/raw/verification-trust-core-tests" \ bun test \ scripts/devtools/consistency.test.ts \ scripts/devtools/receipt-schema.test.ts \ scripts/devtools/privacy.test.ts \ scripts/devtools/receipt-output.test.ts \ scripts/devtools/target-identity.test.ts \ scripts/devtools/glass-observers.test.ts \ scripts/devtools/compare.test.ts Expected: exit 0. Passing tests do not refute the forged-receipt controls; they prove only that checked-in expectations hold. V3 — Remaining DevTools unit tests Bashpython3 "$LANE/00-run-bounded.py" 240 \ "$LANE/raw/verification-devtools-primitive-tests" \ bun test \ scripts/devtools/__tests__/client-lib.test.ts \ scripts/devtools/elements.test.ts \ scripts/devtools/focus.test.ts \ scripts/devtools/layout.test.ts \ scripts/devtools/scroll.test.ts \ scripts/devtools/surface.test.ts \ scripts/devtools/text.test.ts Expected: exit 0. V4 — Catalog Bashpython3 "$LANE/00-run-bounded.py" 60 \ "$LANE/raw/verification-consistency-catalog" \ bun scripts/devtools/consistency.ts catalog \ --fixes .notes/CONSISTENCY-FIXES.md Expected: exit 0, exact 75-task program set and 28-task proof/governance set. V5 — Forged task/program controls Run the two controls from Work package A. Their healthy result is nonzero. An exit 0 is a successful audit reproduction of a critical defect. V6 — Coverage bindings Bashpython3 "$LANE/00-run-bounded.py" 90 \ "$LANE/raw/verification-coverage-bindings" \ bun scripts/devtools/surfaces.ts bindings \ --out "$LANE/raw/coverage-bindings.json" \ --negative-dir "$LANE/raw/coverage-negative" Expected healthy result: exit 0. Record INVALID_SCHEMA as a branch defect, not an environmental failure. V7 — Rendered geometry analyzer Bashpython3 "$LANE/00-run-bounded.py" 60 \ "$LANE/raw/verification-rendered-capsule-geometry" \ python3 -B scripts/agentic/test_rendered_capsule_geometry.py Expected: exit 0. V8 — Source-audit ratchet Bashpython3 "$LANE/00-run-bounded.py" 420 \ "$LANE/raw/verification-source-audit-ratchet" \ ./scripts/agentic/agent-cargo.sh test \ --test source_audit_ratchet Expected: exit 0. Never use bare cargo. V9 — Focused Rust contract tests Bashpython3 "$LANE/00-run-bounded.py" 600 \ "$LANE/raw/verification-rust-contracts" \ ./scripts/agentic/agent-cargo.sh test \ --test agent_chat_cmd_enter_entry_request_contract \ --test dictation_lifecycle_contract \ --test submit_ownership_contract Expected: exit 0. If Cargo rejects repeated --test flags in the local wrapper, record the command error and execute the same three targets as three bounded commands; this is a command correction, not a production fix pass. V10 — Current-HEAD runtime identity proof Build a stable current-HEAD artifact: Bashpython3 "$LANE/00-run-bounded.py" 900 \ "$LANE/raw/verification-build-runtime-artifact" \ env SCRIPT_KIT_AGENT_ARTIFACT_NAME=branch-trust-05 \ ./scripts/agentic/agent-cargo.sh build --bin script-kit-gpui Expected: exit 0 target-agent/artifacts/branch-trust-05/script-kit-gpui exists adjacent manifest sourceCommit equals current HEAD Run the identity probe with output redirected to the lane: Bashpython3 "$LANE/00-run-bounded.py" 300 \ "$LANE/raw/verification-transaction-identity" \ env \ SCRIPT_KIT_GPUI_BINARY=target-agent/artifacts/branch-trust-05/script-kit-gpui \ CONSISTENCY_RECEIPT_PATH="$LANE/raw/transaction-identity.json" \ SCRIPT_KIT_TEST_STATUS=1 \ bun scripts/agentic/cons-proof-gov/transaction-identity-probe.ts Expected: exit 0; exact binary SHA; process start time; target/window generations; lifetime transition checks; zero surviving owned process. V11 — Conditional full glass observer transaction Run this only when an owned checked-in receipt or the article will make a current glass/motion proof claim: Bashpython3 "$LANE/00-run-bounded.py" 1200 \ "$LANE/raw/verification-glass-observers" \ env \ SCRIPT_KIT_TEST_STATUS=1 \ SCRIPT_KIT_RECEIPT_PRIVACY_CANARIES="PT05_CANARY_ALPHA,PT05_CANARY_BETA" \ bun scripts/devtools/glass-observers.ts verify \ --binary target-agent/artifacts/branch-trust-05/script-kit-gpui \ --theme-fixture scripts/agentic/fixtures/glass-motion-calibration-theme.json \ --run-manifest .artifacts/consistency/run.json \ --helper-cache "$LANE/raw/glass-helpers" \ --probes-root "$LANE/raw/glass-probes" \ --out "$LANE/raw/glass-observers.json" Interpret exact exit codes: ExitMeaning0EVALUABLE_PASS2product-level EVALUABLE_FAIL3blocked4invalid observer, identity, fixture, cleanup, or interference One INVALID_INTERFERENCE result permits one quiet-input rerun. It must never be converted to pass. Do not change any calibration value or expected envelope. Rerun rule One diagnosis pass per failed command. One rerun only for a command mistake, explicitly transient environment, or INVALID_INTERFERENCE. No production “fix pass” is authorized. A second failure is recorded and handed to the manager. Record every command in 05-test-runtime-results.md: Markdown| ID | Command receipt | Timeout | Exit | Disposition | Boundary | Rerun | Result | 8. Screenshot requests Write these exact requests to 06-screenshot-requests.md. Capture only when manager-assigned. SS-PT-01 — Actions rendered entry phases Output: .artifacts/branch-trust-audit/05-proof-trust/SS-PT-01-actions-entry-phases.png Source: current-HEAD glass observer/action filmstrip. Frames: first valid visible frame, maximum overshoot/compression, settled frame. Caption: “Three rendered phases of the Actions entry envelope: first visible, maximum overshoot, and settled. The filmstrip illustrates rendered geometry only; input dispatch, lifecycle, interference, and cleanup are established by separate receipts.” Proves: static rendered-frame comparison. Does not prove: keyboard activation, exact timing by itself, focus, cleanup, or absence of interference. SS-PT-02 — Notes-search host parity Output: .artifacts/branch-trust-audit/05-proof-trust/SS-PT-02-notes-search-host-parity.png Panels: Notes-window Actions, Day Page Actions, standalone Notes Browse, Agent Chat attachment portal. Fixture: only the synthetic C08 corpus. Caption: “Four Notes-search hosts show the same ordered synthetic results while preserving host-specific destination verbs. The image is a parity illustration; Enter, second-click activation, cancellation restoration, and stable selection require the runtime receipt.” Proves: visible parity at one captured state. Does not prove: activation, restoration, refresh behavior, or ordering over time. SS-PT-03 — Receipt lineage matrix Output: .artifacts/branch-trust-audit/05-proof-trust/SS-PT-03-receipt-lineage-matrix.png Source: rendered table from raw/receipt-census.tsv. Columns: task binding, registered primitive, producer fingerprint, commit, binary, fixture, evidence layer, negative control, privacy, interference, cleanup. Caption: “Receipt lineage across the audited tasks. Missing trust edges are evidence gaps, not automatic product regressions.” SS-PT-04 — Invalid interference is not green Output: .artifacts/branch-trust-audit/05-proof-trust/SS-PT-04-interference-disposition.png Source: one valid INVALID_INTERFERENCE receipt, when naturally produced. Caption: “Observer interference invalidates the run rather than proving or disproving the product claim.” Do not intentionally disturb the user’s machine merely to manufacture this screenshot. 9. Article and ledger integration hunks Write the final resolved versions to 09-integration-hunks.md. Shared ledger hunk Markdown### 05-proof-trust — Proof and provenance - **Owned scope:** 160/160 paths reviewed. - **Package delta:** 29,425 insertions, 1,209 deletions. - **Verified improvements:** {{IMPROVEMENT_COUNT}}. - **Verified regressions:** {{REGRESSION_COUNT}}. - **Probable defects:** {{PROBABLE_DEFECT_COUNT}}. - **Proof gaps:** {{PROOF_GAP_COUNT}}. - **Unverified prior claims:** {{UNVERIFIED_COUNT}}. - **Highest risk:** {{HIGHEST_RISK_ONE_SENTENCE}}. - **Focused verification:** {{PASS_COUNT}} passed, {{FAIL_COUNT}} failed, {{BLOCKED_COUNT}} blocked, {{INVALID_COUNT}} invalid. - **Runtime boundary:** {{TRANSACTION_IDENTITY_RESULT}}. - **Screenshots requested/captured:** {{SCREENSHOT_SUMMARY}}. - **Evidence root:** `.notes/oracle/branch-trust-audit/lanes/05-proof-trust/`. - **Production edits:** none. Article section heading Markdown## Proof is strongest at the observer and weakest at the admission boundary Opening copy MarkdownThe branch does not merely add tests. It introduces a broad evidence vocabulary: typed dispositions, privacy filtering, target generations, fixture and binary hashes, observer invalidation, negative controls, cleanup receipts, rendered geometry, accessibility joins, and multi-host runtime probes. Several of those changes are genuine improvements because they make failure modes visible that ordinary unit tests cannot see. The audit therefore did not ask only whether the probes were sophisticated. It followed each claim through the last boundary that can still turn evidence into a green program result: receipt creation, schema validation, task binding, staleness checks, aggregation, generated ledgers, and the final checked-in receipts. Conditional decisive paragraph — use exactly one Variant A: forged admission reproduced MarkdownThat final boundary was porous. An audit-only fixture composed of unregistered, identity-free JSON receipts was accepted as {{TASK_OR_PROGRAM_RESULT}}. The receipts did not need a registered primitive, a recognized producer, the correct task identity, a runtime transaction, or a current source commit. They needed only the fields the aggregator counted: a pass disposition, a self-asserted negative control, a self-asserted privacy scan, and closed cleanup. This does not show that the underlying product work is wrong. It shows that the current 75-of-75 style result cannot, by itself, distinguish measured behavior from a sufficiently well-shaped claim. **Confidence:** High. **Evidence:** `.notes/oracle/branch-trust-audit/lanes/05-proof-trust/raw/{{FORGED_RECEIPT}}` and `03-breakage-candidates.md#pt-001`. Variant B: forged admission rejected MarkdownThe final admission boundary rejected the audit’s forged receipts. Unknown producers, incorrect task binding, and incomplete identity could not be promoted into a task or program pass. That result materially increases confidence in the checked-in completion receipts, although individual runtime claims still require their own boundary and freshness review. **Confidence:** High for admission; task-specific confidence remains listed in the evidence matrix. **Evidence:** `.notes/oracle/branch-trust-audit/lanes/05-proof-trust/raw/{{FORGED_RECEIPT}}`. Screenshot caption hunk Markdown> **Figure — Receipt lineage, not just receipt count.** Each row follows a > checked-in claim through producer registration, task binding, source or binary > identity, fixture identity, evidence layers, negative controls, privacy, > interference, and cleanup. A missing edge is labeled unverified rather than > silently counted green. 10. Executable next-steps program This should be the largest substantive section in the published article. PT-N1 — Close receipt admission Priority: P0 Owners: DevTools proof core; consistency verifier. Targets: receipt-schema.ts, consistency.ts, associated tests. Change: validate every stored receipt against a registered primitive, canonical envelope, current registry, and current producer before aggregation. Acceptance: the forged single-task and 75-task controls return nonzero with typed admission errors. Negative control: a valid current receipt still passes unchanged. Evidence after completion: red/green receipts from the exact forged fixtures. Do not: solve this by adding another wrapper receipt that trusts the same unvalidated JSON. PT-N2 — Bind every receipt to its task and source transaction Priority: P0 Owners: Receipt schema and all producers. Targets: taskIds, transaction identity, source/binary commit fields. Change: require the consumed task ID, current source identity, and exact binary/fixture identity where applicable. Acceptance: copying a valid WF receipt into another task directory cannot satisfy the second task. Negative control: an explicitly multi-task shared receipt can satisfy only its declared taskIds. Evidence: task-binding mutation test plus one current runtime receipt. PT-N3 — Enforce evidence-layer requirements Priority: P0 Owners: Receipt registry. Targets: requiredEvidenceLayers, task proof requirements. Change: turn required layers from registry documentation into executable validation. Acceptance: a motion claim without rendered evidence, an activation claim without interaction evidence, or an AX claim without accessibility evidence cannot pass. Negative control: static catalog/path tasks can explicitly declare the appropriate non-runtime layers. Evidence: one mutation test per evidence layer. PT-N4 — Stop manufacturing cleanup and privacy success Priority: P0 Owners: Receipt output/privacy. Targets: normalizedCleanup, recursive canary scan, interference status. Change: default omitted cleanup to unproven, preserve actual recursive-scan receipts, record configured canary count, and require explicit interference applicability. Acceptance: omission produces blocked/invalid status rather than closed:true or performed:true. Negative control: a producer supplying explicit closed cleanup and a nonzero canary scan remains valid. Evidence: minimal targets.list candidate red/green control. PT-N5 — Replace raw governance side channels Priority: P0 Owners: Consistency program integration. Targets: generated-byte compare, conflict lifecycle, façade lifecycle, run manifest. Change: consume registered, identity-bound receipts or derive these facts from existing validated task receipts. Acceptance: empty output hashes, hand-authored counts, or self-asserted façade booleans cannot satisfy verify-all. Negative control: a byte-for-byte exporter receipt with a nonempty complete hash set passes. Evidence: forged-program control. PT-N6 — Verify family members, not family declarations Priority: P0 Owners: Coverage/family verification. Targets: verifyFamily, nine family fixtures. Change: resolve every member path, prevent repository escape, validate every member receipt, and require current passing identities. Acceptance: missing, stale, failed, or wrong-family member receipts block the family. Negative control: all nine real fixtures resolve exactly to their declared current members. Evidence: missing-member mutation and full family census. PT-N7 — Register every producer and primitive Priority: P1 Owners: Receipt registry and DevTools surface inventory. Targets: devtools.coverage.bindings, devtools.glass.observers, script-kit-devtools.surfaces, and any other raw producer found by the census. Change: ensure every task-grade producer emits through the canonical validator. Acceptance: the producer/primitive audit matrix has no unregistered task-grade entries. Negative control: an unknown producer returns INVALID_SCHEMA. Evidence: registry report and surfaces.ts bindings result. PT-N8 — Fingerprint dependency closures, not one file Priority: P1 Owners: Producer identity. Targets: producerSourceFingerprint, imported helper modules, generated source provenance. Change: include the producer’s relevant dependency closure or an exporter-generated implementation manifest. Acceptance: changing privacy.ts, target-identity.ts, or another proof-critical helper stales